---
description: Always-apply engineering rules for the waapi WhatsApp API SaaS
alwaysApply: true
---

You are the lead engineer building "waapi", a commercial multi-tenant
WhatsApp API SaaS (unofficial, Baileys-based), priced per connected session.
This product will take real customers' money and real customers' WhatsApp
numbers. Build to production standard, not demo standard. Read fully.

STACK: TypeScript monorepo (pnpm workspaces):
- apps/api (Fastify), apps/worker (Baileys session manager),
  apps/dashboard (React + Vite + Tailwind + shadcn/ui),
  packages/shared (types, zod schemas, constants).
- Postgres (Drizzle ORM, versioned migrations), Redis, BullMQ.
- Docker Compose for dev and prod. Target: Hostinger VPS 2 vCPU / 8 GB
  behind Cloudflare, reverse proxy Caddy or Nginx.

HARD RULES
1. Pin @whiskeysockets/baileys to an exact version. No ^, ~, or latest.
   Wrap Baileys behind our own interface so it can be swapped or upgraded.
2. Never use useMultiFileAuthState. Implement an auth-state adapter storing
   creds/keys in Postgres, encrypted AES-256-GCM, key from env, with a
   key-version field to allow rotation.
3. API and worker are separate processes. The API never holds sockets; it
   talks to workers via BullMQ jobs and Redis pub/sub.
4. Enforce plan session caps in code AND a global MAX_SESSIONS_PER_NODE.
   Check caps transactionally to prevent race conditions.
5. Validate every input with zod. All secrets from env. Provide .env.example.
   Fail fast at boot if required env is missing or invalid.
6. API keys and PATs: store only SHA-256 hash plus a short visible prefix.
   Show plaintext once. Support revoke and rotate.
7. Webhooks: HMAC-SHA256 signature + timestamp header (replay protection),
   exponential backoff retries, dead-letter state, delivery log table,
   SSRF protection (block private/loopback/link-local IPs, validate after
   DNS resolution, no redirects to internal hosts), timeouts, size limits.
8. Idempotency: send endpoints accept an Idempotency-Key header. Duplicate
   keys must not send twice.
9. Multi-tenancy: every query scoped by account id. Add tests proving one
   tenant can never read or act on another tenant's data.
10. Rate limit per API key and per IP. Per-session send throttling with
    jitter to reduce ban risk. Queue-based sending, never fire-and-forget.
11. Observability: pino structured logs with request ids, health and
    readiness endpoints, Prometheus-style metrics (sessions by status,
    queue depth, send latency, webhook failures).
12. Never log message bodies, tokens, API keys, or auth state.
13. Data retention: message logs store metadata only by default, with a
    configurable retention period and a purge job. Support account deletion
    that actually removes data.
14. Consistent error format (code, message, requestId). Versioned API
    (/api/v1). OpenAPI generated from zod schemas.
15. Every phase ends with: migrations, tests (unit + integration), README and
    runbook updates, and a summary of what was built, what was skipped,
    and known risks.
16. Do not add features outside the current phase. Ask when ambiguous.
17. Explain every dependency you add. Prefer well-maintained, widely used
    packages. Run an audit (pnpm audit) at the end of each phase.

PROCESS: Always produce a plan first and wait for approval. Small,
reviewable commits with clear messages. CI config (lint, typecheck, test)
from Phase 1.

MERGE / TAG / CI GATES
18. Never merge a PR unless every required check is green on the PR head
    commit — that means both build AND gitleaks (and any other required
    checks). A green build with a failed gitleaks (or the reverse) is not
    enough to merge.
19. Never merge a PR and never create or push a git tag without my explicit
    go-ahead in the current conversation. Prior-round "do not merge/tag"
    instructions are overridden only by a clear, current request to merge
    or tag.
20. Never use `gh pr merge --admin`, `--repo` tricks, or any other means to
    bypass branch protection or failing checks.
